WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:04.667
Time, according to Greek philosopher
Theophrastus, is the most valuable

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.708
thing you can spend.

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:11.000
We may want more of it, but we're
stuck with the same 24-hour wad

00:00:11.083 --> 00:00:12.958
every day of our lives.

00:00:13.542 --> 00:00:17.417
Beyoncé's Single Ladies was written
in no more than 20 minutes.

00:00:17.792 --> 00:00:22.458
That's one stonking pop banger
created in less than 2% of a day.

00:00:22.917 --> 00:00:26.833
Imagine what you could
achieve in the other 98%.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.000
The question is, how?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.423
Well, how you think about
time can actually help you use it

00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:34.910
more wisely, and could leave
you a lot healthier and happier.

00:00:36.923 --> 00:00:40.381
You know that rare feeling of having
plenty of time to do what you want?

00:00:40.777 --> 00:00:43.386
Psychologists call it
‘time affluence',

00:00:43.386 --> 00:00:46.219
and it does a lot for
your sense of wellbeing.

00:00:46.897 --> 00:00:50.593
Bizarre as it may sound, it's
possible to make yourself feel more

00:00:50.593 --> 00:00:54.426
time-rich by handing over
some of your hours to others.

00:00:54.715 --> 00:00:56.713
One study took
a large group of people.

00:00:56.713 --> 00:00:59.047
Some of them helped others
in their spare time -

00:00:59.107 --> 00:01:02.302
doing things like writing letters
to cheer up a sick child -

00:01:02.302 --> 00:01:04.469
while the rest did
whatever they liked.

00:01:04.541 --> 00:01:07.781
The helpers later felt they
generally had more free time

00:01:07.781 --> 00:01:10.781
compared to the rest of the group,
despite giving some of it away.

00:01:11.079 --> 00:01:15.621
So rather than bingeing on that box
set, do something for someone else.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:19.249
You'll end up feeling it's possible
to achieve more in the time you have.

00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:24.999
Now, think about all those tiny
chunks of your day when your brain

00:01:24.999 --> 00:01:27.333
isn't doing very much at all.

00:01:27.438 --> 00:01:32.188
Like going for a run, sitting on
a bus, or making a cup of tea.

00:01:32.414 --> 00:01:34.997
Scott H Young,
author of Ultralearning,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.957
specialises in making time
work better for people.

00:01:37.957 --> 00:01:41.416
He sees these mini downtimes
as chunks of opportunity

00:01:41.416 --> 00:01:43.249
to use more wisely.

00:01:43.355 --> 00:01:45.688
So how do you want to use that time?

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:48.313
You could try
‘productive meditation'.

00:01:48.313 --> 00:01:51.688
If something's turning out to
be a pretty knotty problem,

00:01:52.183 --> 00:01:55.433
use your daily jog to focus
your mind on your surroundings,

00:01:55.662 --> 00:01:59.204
and your subconscious may find
a solution while your mind relaxes.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.138
Or while the kettle boils,
listen to the sound of the water.

00:02:03.138 --> 00:02:08.388
Watch steam rising from the spout.
Be truly mindful and in the moment.

00:02:08.388 --> 00:02:10.847
Evidence suggests that when
you get back to your desk,

00:02:11.304 --> 00:02:13.429
you'll get a lot more done
in the time you have.

00:02:13.857 --> 00:02:16.482
“Start creating habits,” Young says.

00:02:16.482 --> 00:02:18.399
“You'll be surprised
how much materializes,

00:02:18.399 --> 00:02:21.649
once you simply start doing
things for a few minutes a day.”

00:02:21.973 --> 00:02:25.223
Whatever route you take, bear in mind
that several recent studies

00:02:25.223 --> 00:02:28.557
have shown that trying to fit
more in by working longer hours

00:02:28.557 --> 00:02:32.182
can lead to stress and illness.
And nobody wants that.

00:02:34.457 --> 00:02:38.249
Psychologist Claudia Hammond suggests
that the best way to make free time

00:02:38.249 --> 00:02:42.083
feel worthwhile is to indulge
in the holiday paradox.

00:02:42.272 --> 00:02:46.022
This says that doing something new
sees your brain make an extra effort

00:02:46.022 --> 00:02:46.939
to remember it.

00:02:46.939 --> 00:02:49.939
So vary your routine,
try new hobbies.

00:02:50.425 --> 00:02:54.258
Different experiences, however small,
will be more rewarding,

00:02:54.488 --> 00:02:58.071
and when you're looking back on them,
your sense of time will be expanded.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.728
Did you realize that if you add up
sleep (very important),

00:03:02.728 --> 00:03:07.093
eating and working, most of us
have around five hours a day left

00:03:07.093 --> 00:03:08.885
to do what we want to do.

00:03:08.885 --> 00:03:10.760
So take advantage of it.

00:03:11.302 --> 00:03:13.135
You could even write
a fifth of a novel.

00:03:13.135 --> 00:03:15.260
John Boyne wrote
The Boy In The Striped Pyjamas

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.635
in just two and a half days

00:03:17.635 --> 00:03:19.321
(not sure how much
sleep he got though).

00:03:19.321 --> 00:03:22.591
Or you could climb to the top
of Ben Nevis in five hours.

00:03:22.841 --> 00:03:25.383
If it was a weekend, you might
have time to get down again.

00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:30.797
But don't be too hard on yourself
if you just need that time to chill.

00:03:30.797 --> 00:03:34.880
A study in 2010 showed that playing
computer games or watching TV

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.307
can restore us emotionally
and help us recover intellectually

00:03:38.307 --> 00:03:39.557
after a hard day.

00:03:39.557 --> 00:03:43.161
But you've got to buy
into the experience totally,

00:03:43.628 --> 00:03:44.878
and don't feel guilty.

00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:46.641
Isn't that a relief?

